  "query_preview": "..\n\nClosed low pressure system continues its trek further east and \neventually swinging through the New England region into tomorrow. \nShowers chances have come to an end though cloud cover will linger \nthrough the remainder of the afternoon supported by the lower level \nmoisture. Drier air will eventually arrive tonight with an inbound \nshortwave ridge leading to a cloud clearing trend. The back edge of \nthe cloud shield can already be seen on satellite arriving into\nwestern portions of the CWA. The clearing skies affords favorable \ncooling conditions and temperatures dipping to around freezing or \nbelow. Coldest overnight lows will be across the Thumb with upper \n20s forecast. \n\nThe Pacific jet will trigger amplification of a shortwave moving \nacross Ontario and into Quebec throughout tomorrow on the heals of \nthe progressive shortwave ridge. Westerly flow develops between \nthese features allowing warm advection to bring temperatures up into \nthe mid-upper 50s range. Afternoon mixing and increasing pressure \ngradient will allow for winds to gusty to around 30 mph at times. \nCanadian shortwave will send a cold front through southeast \nMichigan. The best lift and moisture availability will be tied \ncloser to the center of the low pressure system with bulk of \nprecipitation focused across Lake Huron while parts of the northern \ncarry low end PoPs along and behind the front into Sunday evening. \n\nCold advection/thermal trough is quick to exit Sunday night with \nanother period of warm advection starting Monday morning as 850 mb \ntemperatures climb to around 5C with the arrival of the next ridge. \nThis keeps temperatures on the mild side with mid 50s on Monday and \nclimbing into the low 60s for Tuesday. Dry conditions prevailing \ninto Tuesday as well with surface high pressure holding influence \nover the central Great Lakes. Conditions expected to become more \nunsettled into Wednesday and troughing returns to the region. This \nwill bring the next chances for precipitation and cooler \ntemperatures as long range models point toward phasing systems \ntriggering high amplitude troughing by late week.",
